Maximum vertical height attained by body thrown with velocity $v_1$
h_1=$\frac{v_1^2}{2 g}$Another body of mass $2 \mathrm{~m}$ is projected with a velocity $v_2$ at an angle $\theta$. $\therefore$ Height attained \left(h_2\right)=\frac{v_2^2 \sin ^2 \theta}{2 g}\therefore \quad \frac{h_1}{h_2}=\frac{v_1^2}{v_2^2} \sin ^2 \theta$
But $\quad t_1=2 t_2\therefore \quad \frac{V_2}{g}=2\left[\frac{V_2 \sin \theta}{g}\right]\Rightarrow \quad v_1=2 v_2 \sin \theta\therefore \quad \frac{h_1}{h_2}=\left(\frac{2}{1}\right)^2=\frac{4}{1}
